<p>The masses of these molecules would suggest the opposite trend in their entropies. The observed trend is a result of the more significant variation of entropy with a physical state. At room temperature, I<sub>2</sub> is a solid, Br<sub>2</sub> is a liquid, and Cl<sub>2</sub> is a gas.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/16.E:_Thermodynamics_(Exercises)" class="mt-section"><span id="Q16.2.9"></span><h3 id="Q16.2.9-44122">Q16.2.9</h3>

<p>Consider two processes: sublimation of I<sub>2</sub>(<em>s</em>) and melting of I<sub>2</sub>(<em>s</em>) (Note: the latter process can occur at the same temperature but somewhat higher pressure).</p>

<p class="mt-align-center">\[\ce{I2}(s)&#10230;\ce{I2}(g)\]</p>

<p class="mt-align-center">\[\ce{I2}(s)&#10230;\ce{I2}(l)\]</p>

<p>Is &Delta;<em>S</em> positive or negative in these processes? In which of the processes will the magnitude of the entropy change be greater?</p>

